<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kagoshima</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kagoshima is a city on Kagoshima Bay in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, facing the active volcano Sakurajima. It was the castle town of the Shimazu lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a> landed in 1549, and a British squadron bombarded it in 1863. Satsuma men from Kagoshima led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>, and it is now the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima-prefecture">Kagoshima Prefecture</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kagoshima.jpg" alt="Kagoshima" width="320" height="528" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A pool full of water · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kagoshima_Montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kagoshima lies at the south-western tip of the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a>, on the north-eastern part of the Satsuma Peninsula, and its area includes the whole of Sakurajima. It sits on the bay of the Aira Caldera beneath the stratovolcano Sakurajima and has been nicknamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> of the Eastern world for its bay, warm climate and volcano. Ash from Sakurajima falls constantly on the city, and residents have developed ways of coping, such as schoolchildren wearing helmets against volcanic debris. The kanji of its name mean deer child island, but the source of the name is not clear.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Shimazu Ujihiza of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-clan">Shimazu clan</a> moved into the area around 1340, and the city lies in ancient Satsuma Province, the centre of Shimazu territory from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a> first reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> by landing at Kagoshima on 15 August 1549, on the coast of what is now Gionnosu. Kagoshima was a significant centre of Christian activity until bans were imposed in the late 16th and early 17th centuries.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> (1603–1868) Kagoshima became the seat of the Shimazu's Satsuma Domain, one of the most powerful and wealthiest domains in the country. Satsuma also controlled the semi-independent kingdom of Ryūkyū, and a special embassy building housed Ryūkyūan emissaries in the city. The domain was a centre of anti-Tokugawa sentiment.</p><p>In 1863 the British Royal Navy bombarded Kagoshima to punish the daimyō of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> for the Namamugi Incident, the murder of a British merchant on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokaido">Tōkaidō</a> the year before, for which the domain had refused to pay an indemnity. Many leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> came from Satsuma. Nineteen young men of Satsuma broke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s ban on foreign travel and visited industrial sites in the United Kingdom, and a statue outside the train station honours them. Tōgō Heihachirō, who led the Grand Fleet of the Imperial Japanese Navy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a>, was born in Kagoshima.</p><p>The 1914 eruption of the volcano spread ash across the city with relatively little disruption. On the night of 17 June 1945 120 B-29 bombers dropped incendiary and cluster bombs, destroying 44.1 per cent of the built-up area; the city was targeted for its naval port and its position as a railway terminus. The municipality was founded on 1 April 1889, and on 1 November 2004 five neighbouring towns, among them Sakurajima, were merged into it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kagoshima is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima-prefecture">Kagoshima Prefecture</a> and its largest city by a wide margin, with an estimated 583,966 people in 2024. Its economy centres on light manufacturing, tourism and commercial fishing, and the Kyushu Shinkansen linked it to Hakata Station in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka">Fukuoka</a> in 2011.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kagoshima” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In March 1863 the Imperial Court ordered the expulsion of the barbarians; Chōshū fired on foreign ships, beginning the Shimonoseki campaign, and the British bombarded <b>Kagoshima</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Kagoshima</b>, the heavily outnumbered samurai of Saigō Takamori made a last stand against Imperial Japanese Army troops under Yamagata Aritomo and were annihilated.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-24">24 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Shimazu had fought against Tokugawa Ieyasu at Sekigahara in 1600, but by negotiation they kept Satsuma, Osumi and part of Hyuga, assessed at about 770,000 koku and ruled from <b>Kagoshima</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/satsuma-domain">Satsuma Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Shimazu Takahisa reunited the clan and province by about 1550, ruling from <b>Kagoshima</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/shimazu-clan-domain">Shimazu clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
